10.4  Timestamps: Unix time, 2038 and leap seconds

TL;DR
  - Unix time counts seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC: one number, the same
    everywhere, with no time zone. Most systems store time this way.
  - Signed 32-bit counters overflow on 2038-01-19 at 03:14:07 UTC. 64-bit Linux
    uses 64 bits; old file formats, databases and 32-bit devices may not.
  - For text, use ISO 8601 with an offset: 2026-10-15T09:30:00+02:00.

      date +%s                         # now, as Unix time
      date -d @1700000000              # Unix time to a date (GNU date)
      date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ      # ISO 8601 in UTC

On your machine
  Zone      Europe/Paris
  RTC       the hardware clock keeps UTC
  Source    tsc (also available: hpet, acpi_pm)
  Sync      systemd-timesyncd, synchronised
  Booted    2026-10-11 06:26 UTC

Pitfalls
  - Milliseconds or seconds? 1700000000000 is JavaScript's milliseconds; reading
    it as seconds gives a date in the year 55,000.
  - A date without a time zone ("2026-03-29 02:30") is ambiguous: say which
    zone, or store UTC.
  - ext4 filesystems made with small (128-byte) inodes can't store dates past
    2038; tune2fs -l shows the inode size.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 10.4 --why

Related
  Commands  date
  See also  idk 10.3 Time zones, UTC and daylight saving
  See also  idk 10.1 Wall clock and monotonic clock: measuring time right
